In NestJS a **guard** is a class with `canActivate(context)` that runs before a resolver. It can read metadata that a decorator attached to the handler (through `Reflector`) and the GraphQL arguments of the call. Hoppscotch combines `GqlTeamMemberGuard` with the `@RequiresTeamRole(...)` decorator to say which team roles may call a resolver.

Open `src/team/guards/gql-team-member.guard.ts` and write down, in order:

- each value the guard reads (metadata, user, arguments);
- what it does when each one is missing;
- what decides the final result.

Then read the IDOR section of the validation guide. Its question is: when a call names a resource by ID, which line proves that the caller may touch it? Look at how operations that receive a `collectionID` are protected (`src/team-collection/guards`), and read the first lines of `createCollection` in `team-collection.service.ts` to see which checks run before any database write. Do the same for `verifyAdmin` in `src/auth/auth.service.ts`, and note which HTTP method its route in `auth.controller.ts` uses.
